# Health and public health, stages 0 to 5.
# Notice, compare observations, justify a conclusion from the evidence.
# Products, places and figures are fictional. Nothing here is medical advice.

@ health.0.noticing
goal: Say how you feel, in words or pictures or signs, and know a grown-up you trust who can help.
intro: Your body and your feelings send you messages. You may feel tired, thirsty, hot or worried. You can tell someone with words, with a picture or by pointing. A grown-up you trust can listen and help.
  show: emoji 🥱 🥤 😟
demo: After a busy game, Sam points to a picture of a tired face. He says, "I want a quiet break." His teacher listens and helps him to find a calm place. Sam told someone, and that helped.
  show: flow notice how you feel > tell a grown-up you trust > get help
M: Match each feeling to something you could ask for.
  pairs: Thirsty => A drink of water | Tired => A rest | Cold => A jumper | Worried => A talk with a grown-up
  hint: What helps when you are thirsty?
  why: Telling someone how you feel helps you to get what you need.
P: Sam cannot find the words to say that he is tired. How else can he show it?
  + Point to a picture of a tired face :: A picture can say it for him.
  - Hide under the table :: [did-not-tell] Then nobody knows how he feels.
  - Push a friend :: [unkind-choice] That hurts someone, and does not tell anyone that he is tired.
  hint: You can tell people things without using words.
  why: Words, pictures, pointing and signs are all good ways to say how you feel.
P: Two children go on the same swing. Mia feels excited. Tom feels worried. Is one of them wrong?
  + No: people can feel differently about the same thing :: Both feelings are real.
  - Yes: Tom is wrong to feel worried :: [one-right-feeling] There is no one right feeling.
  - Yes: Mia is wrong to feel excited :: [one-right-feeling] There is no one right feeling.
  hint: Do you and your friends always feel the same way?
  why: The same thing can make different people feel different ways. That is all right.
>P: Your tummy feels funny at school. What is a good thing to do?
  + Tell your teacher how you feel :: A grown-up you trust can help.
  - Keep it a secret :: [did-not-tell] Nobody can help if nobody knows.
  - Run away and hide :: [did-not-tell] Then nobody can find you to help.
  hint: Who helps you at school?
  why: When something feels wrong, tell a grown-up you trust. You do not have to work it out alone.
done: Notice, name it, and ask. Who are two grown-ups you trust?

@ health.0.caring
goal: Know the people and places that help us, and help everyone to join in.
intro: People and places help us to feel well. A quiet corner helps us to rest. A water fountain gives us a drink. A grown-up who listens helps when we are sad. Needing help is never something to be ashamed of.
  show: emoji 🚰 📚 🤗
demo: A girl in a wheelchair wants to get to the reading corner. Bags on the floor are in her way. The children put the bags on their pegs. Now she can get there. The children changed the room, not the girl.
  show: flow bags in the way > move the bags > everyone can reach the books
M: Match each need to a place or person that helps.
  pairs: A drink => The water fountain | A rest => The quiet corner | Someone to listen => A grown-up you trust | A game => The playground
  hint: Where do you go to rest?
  why: Different places and people help us in different ways.
G: Does it help everyone to join in, or keep some people out?
  bin Helps everyone join in: a clear path | a seat in the shade | toys on a low shelf
  bin Keeps some people out: bags left in the way | books on a shelf too high to reach | a door too heavy to open
  hint: Could every child use it?
  why: When we make a space easy to use, everyone can join in.
P: A friend cannot reach the water fountain. It is too high. What is a kind and helpful thing to do?
  + Ask a grown-up for a step or a cup :: Then your friend can have a drink.
  - Tell your friend they do not need a drink :: [changed-the-person-not-the-place] Your friend is thirsty. The fountain is the problem.
  - Laugh :: [unkind-choice] That is unkind, and does not help.
  hint: Is the trouble your friend, or how high the fountain is?
  why: When a place is hard to use, we can change the place.
>P: A new boy wants to join the game, but he cannot run fast. What can the children do?
  + Change the game so that everyone can play :: A new rule can let everyone in.
  - Tell him to watch :: [left-someone-out] He wants to play too.
  - Tell him to run faster :: [changed-the-person-not-the-game] The game can change more easily than he can.
  hint: What did the children do about the bags?
  why: Caring means changing things so that nobody is left out.
done: People and places help us. Look for one thing in your room that could be easier for someone to use.

@ health.1.messages
goal: Find what a health message means and what it asks you to do, and ask when you do not understand.
intro: Signs, letters and announcements tell us things about keeping well. A good message says what it means and what to do. If a message is hard to understand, that is the message's fault, not yours.
  show: flow who wrote it? > what is it about? > what should I do? > check with a grown-up
demo: A sign says, "Hydration station unavailable." That is hard to read. A better sign says, "This drinking fountain is closed. Ask your teacher where to get water." It uses plain words and tells you what to do next.
  show: table Hard to read | Easy to read ;; Hydration station unavailable. | This drinking fountain is closed. Ask your teacher where to get water.
C: Two signs say the same thing. Which is the better message?
  left: text Hydration station unavailable.
  right: text This drinking fountain is closed. Ask your teacher where to get water.
  + The right: it uses plain words and tells you what to do :: You know what has happened, and what to do next.
  - The left: long words are more correct :: [long-words-are-better] A message is good when people can understand it.
  - They are just as good as each other :: [same-meaning-same-quality] They mean the same, but only one is easy to use.
  hint: Which one could a younger child understand?
  why: A good message names the thing, says what has happened and tells you what to do.
E: Make this sign easier to understand.
  tokens: Please | perform hand hygiene{wash your hands,consider hygiene} | before lunch.
  limit: 1
  aim: Change one part so that everyone knows what to do.
  accept: 2=wash your hands
  x 2=consider hygiene :: [still-unclear] That is still hard to understand, and it does not say what to do.
  hint: What does "perform hand hygiene" mean in plain words?
  why: "Wash your hands" is short and clear, and it tells you exactly what to do.
P: You see a word on a sign that you do not understand. What should you do?
  + Ask a grown-up you trust what it means :: Asking is the right thing to do.
  - Guess, and tell nobody :: [guessed-alone] You might guess wrong.
  - Feel bad about not knowing :: [blamed-yourself] The sign should have used a plainer word.
  hint: Who can help you with a hard word?
  why: Asking is how we learn. Hard words are a reason to make the sign better.
>P: A letter from school says, "Commencing Monday, pupils must utilise the east entrance." What is the main thing it asks you to do?
  + From Monday, come in by the east door :: "Commencing" means starting, and "utilise" means use.
  - Stay at home on Monday :: [misread-the-action] It says which door to use. It does not say to stay away.
  - Bring a pencil on Monday :: [not-in-the-message] The letter does not mention pencils.
  hint: Find what the letter asks pupils to do. "Utilise" means "use".
  why: Look for the action in a message, then say it in your own words and check it with a grown-up.
done: What does it mean, and what should I do? Find a sign near you and say it in plainer words.

@ health.1.shared-spaces
goal: Notice what helps and what gets in the way in a shared space, and ask the people who use it.
intro: A shared space is a place that many people use, such as a playground or a library. Some things in it help people to join in. Some things get in the way. When something gets in the way, we can change the space.
  show: chips shade | seats | a clear path | signs you can read | water to drink
demo: A class counts the benches in the playground. Four are in the sun and one is in the shade. That is something they saw. It does not tell them where each child likes to sit. To find that out, they ask the children.
  show: bars In_the_sun=4 In_the_shade=1 unit=benches
G: Is it something you saw, or a guess about people?
  bin Something you saw: There is one bench in the shade. | The fountain is not working. | The sign is too high to read from a wheelchair.
  bin A guess about people: Nobody likes to sit in the sun. | The children here do not like water. | He never reads the signs.
  hint: Could you check it by looking?
  why: What we see tells us about the space. To learn what people need, we have to ask them.
P: The path into the garden has a big step. A girl in a wheelchair cannot get in. Where is the problem?
  + In the path: the step is in the way :: The space can be changed.
  - In the girl :: [blamed-the-person] She wants to visit the garden. The step is what stops her.
  - There is no problem :: [ignored-the-barrier] A child who wants to come in cannot.
  hint: What could be changed so that she can get in?
  why: When someone cannot use a space, look first at the space.
P: The class wants to make the playground better. Whom should they ask?
  + The children who use it, and those who find parts of it hard to use :: They know what gets in the way.
  - Only the tallest children :: [left-people-out] Everyone who uses it should have a say.
  - Nobody: just guess :: [guessed-not-asked] Asking tells you things that you cannot see.
  hint: Who knows what it is like to use the playground?
  why: The people who use a space know most about it, and those who have trouble using it know most of all.
>P: Many children do not drink water at school. One of the two fountains is broken, and the other is a long way from the classrooms. What is a helpful idea?
  + Mend the fountain, and put water nearer to the classrooms :: Change the space.
  - Tell the children off for not drinking :: [blamed-individuals] It is hard for them to get water.
  - Do nothing :: [ignored-the-barrier] The trouble will still be there.
  hint: Why might it be hard to get a drink?
  why: When many people have the same trouble, look for what is in the way and change it.
done: Look, ask, then change the space. Find one thing that helps and one thing that gets in the way in a place you know.

@ health.2.claims
goal: Separate a health claim from the evidence for it, and ask what an advertisement leaves out.
intro: A claim says that something is true. Evidence is the information that supports it. A single story is the weakest kind of evidence: it cannot show what would have happened anyway. The products in this lesson are invented.
  show: table Kind | Example ;; a claim | "FocusBand improves quiz scores." ;; a story | "One pupil scored higher after wearing it." ;; a fair comparison | two similar groups, with and without it, given the same quiz
demo: A poster says that a wristband called FocusBand improves quiz scores, because one pupil scored higher after wearing it. But she may also have studied harder, or the quiz may have been easier. One story cannot separate the wristband from everything else that changed.
  show: flow one pupil wore it > she scored higher > was it the band, the studying or the quiz?
G: A claim, a single story, or a fair comparison?
  bin Claim: FocusBand improves quiz scores.
  bin Single story: One pupil scored higher after wearing it. | My cousin says that it worked for her.
  bin Fair comparison: Two similar classes took the same quiz, one with the band and one without. | A hundred wearers and a hundred non-wearers were tested in the same way.
  hint: Does it state something, tell one person's experience, or compare groups?
  why: A claim needs evidence. Stories are weak evidence. Comparisons between similar groups are far stronger.
P: A study that praises a snack bar was paid for by the company that makes it. What follows?
  + Look closely at how the study was done; the payment is a reason for care, not proof that the study is wrong :: An interest is a warning sign.
  - The study must be false :: [interest-as-proof] The source's interest does not settle whether the result is true.
  - Nothing: it makes no difference who paid :: [ignored-the-interest] Those who pay may choose what gets measured and reported.
  hint: Does knowing who paid tell you whether the result is right?
  why: A financial interest calls for a closer look at the methods. It is not evidence about the result.
K: Does an invented drink called ZipJuice improve concentration? Turn over as few facts as you need to decide what the evidence shows.
  clue Advert: The advertisement says, "Scientifically proven!"
  clue Maker: The company earns money from every bottle sold.
  clue Comparison: In a fair comparison of 200 pupils, those given ZipJuice and those given a look-alike drink did equally well on the same task.
  clue Athlete: A well-known athlete says that she loves it.
  need: 3
  + The evidence does not show that it helps :: A fair comparison found no difference.
  - It helps: the advertisement says that it is proven :: [impressive-words] "Scientifically proven" is a slogan until the study is shown.
  - It cannot help, because the company profits :: [interest-as-proof] Making a profit does not make a product useless. The comparison is what tells us.
  hint: Which fact compares people who had the drink with people who did not?
  why: Slogans, profits and admirers are not evidence about what the product does. A fair comparison is.
>P: A poster for an invented cereal says, "9 out of 10 people felt more energetic!" What should you ask before believing it?
  + How many were asked, compared with whom, how "energetic" was measured, and who ran the survey :: The poster leaves all of that out.
  - Nothing: nine out of ten is convincing :: [number-as-proof] Nine out of ten of whom, compared with what?
  - Nothing: all advertisements are false :: [blanket-dismissal] Some claims are true. The point is to check.
  hint: What would you need to know to judge the figure?
  why: A number without its comparison group, its measurement and its source cannot be judged.
done: What is the claim, and what is the evidence? Find an advertisement and ask what it leaves out.

@ health.2.population-data
goal: Read simple tables about groups of people, using the denominator, the time period and care for privacy.
intro: Data about a population describe a group, not a person. A count means little by itself: you need to know out of how many. Ten out of twenty is half; ten out of two hundred is one in twenty. The surveys here are invented.
  show: table Group | Said yes | Asked | Share ;; A | 15 | 25 | 60% ;; B | 24 | 60 | 40%
demo: In an invented survey, 15 of the 25 learners in group A, and 24 of the 60 in group B, say that they can reach a quiet place to study. B has more people saying yes, but A has the larger share: 60% against 40%. To compare groups of different sizes, compare shares.
  show: bars Group_A=60 Group_B=40 unit=%
F: Work out a share.
  frame: 15 out of 25 is __ %
  answer: 60
  x 15 :: [count-as-percentage] Fifteen is the count. Divide it by 25, then multiply by 100.
  x 40 :: [counted-the-others] Forty per cent is the share who did not say yes.
  hint: 15 ÷ 25, then × 100.
  why: 15 ÷ 25 = 0.6, which is 60%.
X: Twelve people in a group say yes. Change the size of the group and watch the share.
  param n: 20..120 /10 @20 | People in the group
  out share: 1200/n | Share saying yes | %
  aim: Find the size of group for which twelve people make up 20%.
  target: share == 20
  view: bars share over n
  hint: The same count is a smaller share of a larger group.
  why: 12 ÷ 60 = 20%. The count never changed. Only the denominator did, and the share fell from 60% to 10% across the dial.
M: Match each part of a survey to an example.
  pairs: Population => The learners at one invented school | Question => "Can you reach a quiet place to study?" | Time period => During the spring term | Denominator => The 25 learners who answered
  hint: The denominator is the number you divide by.
  why: A figure can be understood only when its population, question, time period and denominator are stated.
P: A table of survey results has one row that contains a single person. Why is that a problem?
  + Readers might work out who it is :: Very small groups can reveal individuals.
  - It is not: tables never identify anyone :: [aggregate-means-anonymous] A group of one is a person.
  - One person is not worth counting :: [dismissed-the-person] The person counts. The concern is their privacy.
  hint: If you knew the school, could you guess who it was?
  why: Results for very small groups are usually combined or withheld, to protect privacy.
>P: In an invented report, town A has 30 cases of something and town B has 60. Is B worse off?
  + It cannot be said without each town's population and the same time period :: Thirty in 1,000 is more than sixty in 6,000.
  - Yes: 60 is more than 30 :: [count-without-denominator] The towns may differ greatly in size.
  - No: the towns are the same :: [assumed-equal] Nothing has been said about their sizes.
  hint: Out of how many?
  why: Counts must be turned into shares before groups of different sizes can be compared.
done: Always ask "out of how many, and over what period?" Find a number in the news and look for its denominator.

@ health.3.epidemiology
goal: Distinguish prevalence from incidence, calculate a risk difference and a risk ratio, and keep association apart from causation.
intro: Epidemiology studies how health-related states are distributed in populations. Prevalence counts the existing cases at a given time. Incidence counts the new cases arising among people at risk over a period. All the figures here are invented.
  show: table Measure | Counts | Among ;; prevalence | existing cases at a given time | everyone in the population ;; cumulative incidence | new cases over a period | those at risk at the start
demo: Two invented groups of 300 people are followed for a year. In the exposed group 45 develop the outcome: a risk of 15%. In the unexposed group 30 do: a risk of 10%. The risk difference is 5 percentage points, and the risk ratio is 1.5. Both figures describe the same data.
  show: table Group | New cases | At risk | Risk ;; exposed | 45 | 300 | 15% ;; unexposed | 30 | 300 | 10%
G: Prevalence or incidence?
  bin Prevalence: On 1 March, 120 of 2,000 residents had the condition. | This term, 8% of pupils wear glasses.
  bin Incidence: Of 1,000 people free of the condition in January, 25 had developed it by December. | Forty new cases arose in a year among 800 workers at risk.
  hint: Existing cases at one time, or new cases over a period?
  why: Prevalence is a snapshot. Incidence is a record of new events among people who could have them.
F: Calculate a risk.
  frame: 45 new cases among 300 people at risk is a risk of __ %
  answer: 15
  x 45 :: [count-as-percentage] Forty-five is the count. Divide it by 300.
  x 6.7 :: [inverted-the-ratio] Divide the cases by the people at risk, not the other way round.
  hint: 45 ÷ 300, then × 100.
  why: 45 ÷ 300 = 0.15, or 15%.
F: Compare the two risks by their ratio.
  frame: A risk of 15% compared with a risk of 10% gives a risk ratio of __
  answer: 1.5
  x 5 :: [difference-for-ratio] Five percentage points is the risk difference. The ratio divides one risk by the other.
  x 0.67 :: [inverted-the-ratio] That is 10 ÷ 15. The question compares 15% with 10%.
  hint: 15 ÷ 10.
  why: 15 ÷ 10 = 1.5. The exposed group's risk is one and a half times that of the unexposed group.
C: Two descriptions of the same invented result.
  left: text "The risk is halved!"
  right: text "The risk falls from 2 in 10,000 to 1 in 10,000."
  + They are the same result: the left gives the relative change and the right gives the absolute one, which shows how small it is :: One fewer case in every 10,000 people.
  - The left describes a bigger effect :: [relative-sounds-larger] It only sounds bigger. The result is the same.
  - They contradict each other :: [measures-confused] Halving 2 in 10,000 gives 1 in 10,000.
  hint: Halve 2 in 10,000.
  why: A relative change can sound large when the absolute change is tiny. Good reporting gives both.
>P: In the invented data, the risk in the exposed group is 1.5 times that in the unexposed group. Does the exposure cause the outcome?
  + That is not established: the groups may differ in other ways, and the design must be examined first :: Association is not yet causation.
  - Yes: the ratio is above 1 :: [association-as-causation] A ratio describes the data. It does not explain them.
  - No: the exposure certainly has no effect :: [overcorrection] The data do not rule out a cause. They cannot establish one alone.
  hint: What else might differ between the two groups?
  why: Measures of association describe. A causal conclusion needs attention to how people came to be in each group, how the outcome was measured and who was studied.
done: Existing or new; difference or ratio; described or caused. Find a risk reported in the news and look for both the absolute and the relative figure.

@ health.3.determinants
goal: Explain how social and environmental conditions shape health, tell equal provision from equitable access and judge who benefits from a policy.
intro: The chance of good health is shaped by housing, schooling, work, transport and surroundings as well as by what individuals do. These "upstream" conditions affect whole groups. A difference between groups is an inequality. Whether it is unfair is a further question, about whether it could be avoided.
  show: flow conditions: housing, transport, air > opportunities > choices > outcomes
demo: A city offers online booking for a free community activity. Attendance rises overall. But residents without reliable internet attend less than before. Everyone was offered the same thing, yet access was not the same. Adding booking by phone and in person removes the barrier.
  show: table Provision | Who can use it ;; online booking only | people with reliable internet ;; online, by phone and in person | nearly everyone
G: An upstream condition, or an individual choice?
  bin Upstream condition: the quality of housing | whether buses serve the district | the air near a main road | the distance to the nearest park
  bin Individual choice: what someone chooses for lunch | whether someone walks or drives a short distance | when someone goes to bed
  hint: Could one person change it alone?
  why: Upstream conditions set the options. Individual choices are made among the options that people actually have.
C: A city wants every resident to be able to book a free activity. Which approach is equitable?
  left: text Send every household the same online booking link.
  right: text Offer booking online, by phone and in person.
  + The right: it removes a barrier for people without internet, so that access is real for more of them :: The same provision is not the same access.
  - The left: everyone receives exactly the same :: [equal-means-fair] Identical provision leaves existing barriers untouched.
  - Neither: people should try harder :: [blamed-individuals] Effort does not supply an internet connection.
  hint: Who cannot use an online link?
  why: Equality gives everyone the same thing. Equity asks what each group needs in order to reach the same opportunity.
B: Choose the three steps that belong in a fair assessment of a public-health proposal.
  bank: Define the problem together with the people affected | Find out who benefits and who bears the costs | Check whether barriers differ between groups | Decide alone and announce the result | Assume that everyone starts from the same place
  answer: Define the problem together with the people affected | Find out who benefits and who bears the costs | Check whether barriers differ between groups
  any-order
  x Decide alone and announce the result :: [excluded-the-community] The people affected know things that planners do not.
  x Assume that everyone starts from the same place :: [assumed-equal-starting-points] Different starting points are exactly what must be checked.
  hint: A fair assessment involves the people affected and looks at the distribution.
  why: Fairness depends on who was heard, on who gains and who pays, and on whether the barriers fall evenly.
>P: A city opens a free wellbeing centre in its middle. Overall attendance is high, but it is low among residents of outlying districts with few buses. How should the policy be judged?
  + The average looks good but the benefits are unevenly spread: transport is a barrier that the plan did not address :: Look at who is missing.
  - It is a success: average attendance is high :: [average-hides-distribution] An average can hide a group that is left out.
  - Those residents do not care about their wellbeing :: [blamed-individuals] They face a barrier that others do not.
  hint: Who is missing from the average?
  why: A public-health policy is judged by its distribution as well as by its average: who benefits, who does not, and why.
done: Look upstream, and ask who is left out. Choose one local service and list the barriers to using it.

@ health.4.causal-inference
goal: State a causal question precisely, explain what random assignment does and does not achieve, and identify confounding, selection and measurement bias.
intro: A causal question asks what would differ if the same people experienced one course of action and not another. Because nobody can live both versions, the answer has to come from comparison, and every method of comparison rests on assumptions.
  show: table Part of the question | It states ;; population | whom the question is about ;; alternatives | the two courses of action compared ;; outcome | what is measured ;; time horizon | over what period
demo: Residents who choose to join a walking group already live nearer to parks and report better wellbeing before they join. Comparing joiners with non-joiners mixes the group's effect with those differences. Inviting residents at random makes the two groups alike on average before the programme begins, so that a later difference can be attributed to the invitation.
  show: flow nearness to parks > joins the group ;; nearness to parks > wellbeing
M: Match each part of a causal question to what it states.
  pairs: Population => Whom the question is about | Alternatives => The courses of action being compared | Outcome => What is measured | Time horizon => The period over which it is measured
  hint: A causal question compares two alternatives for the same population.
  why: Until all four are stated, there is no definite question for a study to answer.
G: Which kind of bias is it?
  bin Confounding: People who joined already had higher incomes. | Joiners lived nearer to parks before they joined.
  bin Selection bias: Only those who completed the programme were analysed. | The survey reached only people with internet access.
  bin Measurement bias: Staff who knew each person's group rated the outcome. | The two groups were asked about wellbeing in different words.
  hint: Is the trouble a common cause, who ended up in the data, or how the outcome was recorded?
  why: Confounding comes from common causes, selection bias from who is included, and measurement bias from how the data are recorded.
P: What does random assignment achieve?
  + It balances the earlier causes of the outcome between the groups, on average :: Known and unknown alike.
  - It guarantees that everyone follows the assigned programme :: [randomisation-fixes-everything] People may still decline or drop out.
  - It removes errors of measurement :: [randomisation-fixes-everything] How outcomes are measured is a separate matter.
  hint: What does chance do to the differences between the groups before the programme?
  why: Randomisation deals with confounding. Non-adherence, missing data and measurement problems remain to be handled.
K: A study estimates the total effect on wellbeing of being invited to a walking programme. Should the analysis adjust for each person's weekly activity? Turn over as few facts as you need to decide.
  clue Timing: Activity was measured three months after the invitation, and the programme is designed to change it.
  clue Association: Activity is strongly associated with wellbeing.
  clue Completeness: Activity was recorded for every participant.
  clue Instrument: Activity was measured with a step counter.
  need: 1
  + Do not adjust: activity lies on the path from the invitation to wellbeing :: Adjusting would remove part of the very effect being measured.
  - Adjust: it predicts the outcome :: [adjust-for-everything] Predicting the outcome is not enough. What matters is when the variable arises, and what causes it.
  hint: Is activity a cause of joining, or a result of it?
  why: Adjust for the common causes of the exposure and the outcome, not for the consequences of the exposure. Adjusting for a variable on the causal path blocks part of the effect.
>P: Residents who choose a cycling scheme report better wellbeing six months later than they did before. Why can this comparison not show the effect of the scheme?
  + Those who chose it differ from those who did not, and wellbeing can change with the season and with time :: There is no measure of what would have happened without the scheme.
  - It can: wellbeing rose after they joined :: [before-after-as-causal] A change that follows something is not shown to be caused by it.
  - Because wellbeing cannot be measured :: [dismissed-the-outcome] It can be measured. The missing piece is the comparison.
  hint: What would have happened to these same people over six months without the scheme?
  why: A causal claim needs an estimate of the alternative. A comparison of before with after supplies none.
done: State the question, then ask what the comparison assumes. Take one causal claim from the news and write its four parts.

@ health.4.appraisal
goal: Judge a study's bias separately from its precision, interpret an estimate with its interval, and plan a transparent synthesis of evidence.
intro: Critical appraisal asks two different questions of a study. Is it biased: does its method push the answer one way? And is it precise: how much would the answer vary by chance? A very large study can be precisely wrong.
  show: table Property | Depends on | Improved by ;; precision | the size of the sample and the variability | more data ;; freedom from bias | who was studied and how | better design
demo: To estimate the share of residents who walk every day, one team surveys 50,000 volunteers recruited through a fitness app. Another surveys 800 residents chosen at random. The first gives a very narrow interval around the wrong answer, because users of a fitness app walk more than most people. The second gives a wider interval around the right one.
  show: table Study | Sample | Problem ;; fitness-app volunteers | 50,000 | precise but biased ;; random sample of residents | 800 | less precise but unbiased
C: Which study better estimates the share of all residents who walk every day?
  left: text 50,000 volunteers recruited through a fitness app
  right: text 800 residents chosen at random
  + The right: it represents the residents, and a huge biased sample only makes the wrong answer more exact :: Size does not cure bias.
  - The left: it is far larger :: [size-as-quality] Its members differ systematically from the residents as a whole.
  - Neither can tell us anything :: [gave-up] A random sample of 800 gives a usable estimate.
  hint: Who signs up to a fitness app?
  why: Precision and bias are separate. More data shrink the random error, but not the systematic error.
G: Does it affect precision, or bias?
  bin Precision: the size of the sample | how variable the outcome is
  bin Bias: who was selected for the study | how the outcome was measured | which participants dropped out
  hint: Would simply collecting more data of the same kind put it right?
  why: Collecting more data in the same way improves precision, and leaves bias exactly where it was.
P: A study reports an improvement of 12 points, with a 95% interval from 2 to 22. How should that be read?
  + The data are compatible with a small or a large improvement, under the study's assumptions; bias is not included in the interval :: An interval describes chance, not design.
  - The true effect is exactly 12 :: [point-estimate-as-truth] Twelve is the best single estimate. The interval shows the uncertainty.
  - The interval covers every possible error :: [interval-covers-bias] It reflects sampling variation only.
  hint: What kind of uncertainty does an interval describe?
  why: An interval expresses statistical uncertainty under a model. Flaws in the design are a separate matter.
O: Put the stages of a systematic review in order.
  items: State the question and the eligibility criteria | Search transparently | Screen the studies against the criteria | Assess each study's risk of bias | Combine the results that are compatible | Report the limits and the certainty
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The criteria are fixed before the searching begins.
  why: Fixing the question and the criteria first prevents studies from being chosen for the sake of their results.
>P: A review pools twelve studies and reports a benefit. Ten are small and loosely conducted, and all of them are positive. Two are large and carefully conducted, and both find no effect. How should the pooled result be read?
  + With caution: the average may reflect bias in the small studies, and the careful studies carry more weight :: Quality matters more than the count.
  - As established: the pooled estimate is positive :: [pooling-as-proof] Pooling biased studies gives a biased average.
  - As established: ten studies beat two :: [vote-counting] Studies are weighed by their quality, not counted.
  hint: Which studies are least likely to be biased?
  why: A synthesis is only as sound as the studies in it. Small, weak studies that all point one way are a warning sign.
done: Biased or not, precise or not: two separate questions. Read the methods section of a study before its conclusion.

@ health.5.evaluation
goal: Design an evaluation of a public-health programme that covers impact, implementation and equity, and criticise the assumptions behind its causal estimate.
intro: A programme evaluation asks more than "did it work?" It asks whether the programme was delivered as intended, whom it reached, and whether the change can be credited to it. A finding of no average effect has several possible explanations, and telling them apart is the evaluator's task.
  show: table Question | Kind of evidence ;; did outcomes change because of it? | a comparison with a credible alternative ;; was it delivered as planned? | records of implementation ;; whom did it reach and whom did it miss? | data by group, and interviews
demo: A city builds shaded waiting areas in some districts. A comfort score there rises from 50 to 62. In comparison districts it rises from 48 to 54. The change in the districts with shelters, 12, less the change in the comparison districts, 6, gives an estimate of 6. That estimate assumes that both sets of districts would otherwise have followed the same trend.
  show: table Districts | Before | After | Change ;; with shelters | 50 | 62 | 12 ;; comparison | 48 | 54 | 6
F: Calculate the difference in differences from the table.
  frame: The estimated effect of the shelters is __ points
  answer: 6 | six
  x 12 :: [ignored-the-comparison] Twelve is the raw change. Subtract the change in the comparison districts.
  x 8 :: [compared-the-endpoints] 62 − 54 ignores the fact that the districts started at different levels.
  hint: Take the change with shelters, and subtract the change without.
  why: 12 − 6 = 6. The comparison districts estimate what would have happened anyway.
P: The estimate from a difference in differences rests on one key assumption. Which?
  + That without the programme both groups would have followed parallel trends :: This can be made plausible, but it cannot be proved.
  - That the two groups began at the same level :: [equal-levels-assumed] The levels may differ. It is the trends that must match.
  - That the result is statistically significant :: [significance-as-assumption] A p-value says nothing about whether the comparison is valid.
  hint: What do the comparison districts stand in for?
  why: The comparison group supplies the counterfactual trend. Similar trends beforehand support the assumption without proving it.
G: Which kind of evaluation question is it?
  bin Impact: Did comfort scores change more than they otherwise would have?
  bin Implementation: Were the shelters built as designed, and kept in repair? | How many of the planned districts received them?
  bin Equity: Who could not use the shelters, and why? | Did the benefit differ between districts?
  hint: Did it work, was it delivered, and for whom?
  why: A complete evaluation asks all three. Each can explain a result that the others cannot.
S: An evaluation finds no average effect. Select every explanation that must be considered.
  + The idea does not work :: A real possibility.
  + The programme reached too few people :: Low reach dilutes any effect.
  + It was delivered poorly :: A good idea badly carried out.
  + The outcome that was measured did not capture the benefit :: The wrong yardstick.
  + The study was too imprecise to detect an effect :: Absence of evidence.
  - The programme has been proved harmful :: [null-as-harm] Finding no average effect is not evidence of harm.
  hint: A null result has many possible causes.
  why: "No effect found" could mean a failure of theory, of reach, of delivery, of measurement or of precision. An evaluation is designed to tell these apart.
>P: The estimate for the shelters shows a small average gain. Interviews reveal that several shelters had steps and no ramp, and that others were left unrepaired. How do the two kinds of evidence fit together?
  + The interviews explain how the programme was delivered and who was excluded, and help to interpret the estimate without replacing it :: Each answers a different question.
  - The interviews overrule the numbers :: [qualitative-overrides] They explain the result. They do not measure the average effect.
  - The interviews are anecdotes and should be ignored :: [dismissed-qualitative] They reveal failures of delivery and of access that the numbers cannot show.
  hint: What can interviews tell you that an average cannot?
  why: Mixed methods combine an estimate of impact with an account of why it came out as it did. Both are needed if the programme is to be improved.
done: Impact, delivery and fairness, each with its own evidence. Sketch an evaluation plan for one local programme.

@ health.5.research
goal: Review a public-health research protocol: the fit of question to analysis, reproducibility with privacy, and claims kept within what the design supports.
intro: A defensible study aligns its question, data, design and analysis before any outcomes are seen. A protocol fixes what will count as a test, how missing data will be treated and what will be reported. Openness makes the work checkable, but it must be weighed against the participants' privacy.
  show: flow question > design > analysis plan fixed in advance > ethics review > data > report
demo: A team studies access to green space, using survey answers and home locations. Publishing the exact coordinates would make the work easy to reproduce, and could expose where each participant lives. A responsible plan shares the analysis code, synthetic demonstration data and a documented, controlled route to the sensitive data.
  show: table Shared openly | Kept under control ;; the analysis code | exact home locations ;; synthetic demonstration data | answers that could identify a person
G: Confirmatory or exploratory?
  bin Confirmatory: The hypothesis and its analysis were written down before the outcomes were seen. | The primary outcome was named in the registered protocol.
  bin Exploratory: A pattern in one subgroup was noticed after the data had been examined. | An extra outcome was analysed because the first result looked interesting.
  hint: Was the test fixed before the results were known?
  why: A confirmatory claim needs a test fixed in advance. Patterns found afterwards are hypotheses for the next study.
K: A report highlights a benefit in one subgroup. Turn over as few facts as you need to decide whether that finding can be presented as confirmatory.
  clue Protocol: The registered protocol specifies only an analysis of the whole sample.
  clue Result: The subgroup result has a p-value of 0.03.
  clue Direction: The result is in the direction that the authors expected.
  clue Search: The subgroup was one of twenty examined after the data had been seen.
  need: 1 | 4
  + No: it is exploratory, and should be reported as a hypothesis to be tested :: It was not fixed in advance, or it was found by searching.
  - Yes: the p-value is below 0.05 :: [significance-as-confirmation] Among twenty subgroups, one such result is expected by chance alone.
  hint: Was this analysis chosen before, or after, the data were seen?
  why: A p-value has its usual meaning only for a test fixed beforehand. Searching many subgroups makes striking results likely by chance.
P: The team wants its study to be reproducible, but the data include home locations. What is the responsible plan?
  + Share the code, synthetic data and documentation, with controlled access to the sensitive data for approved researchers :: Reproducibility together with protection.
  - Publish everything, the exact locations included :: [openness-over-privacy] That could expose the participants.
  - Share nothing :: [privacy-as-secrecy] Then nobody can check the work, although much could safely be shared.
  hint: What can be shared without identifying anyone?
  why: Openness and privacy are both obligations. Careful design can meet most of both.
P: In a study, 30% of outcomes are missing, and more are missing in one group than in the other. Why is it risky to analyse only the complete cases?
  + If missingness is related to the outcome, the remaining cases are not representative, and the comparison is biased :: Those who dropped out may differ.
  - It is not risky: the missing data are simply lost :: [missing-as-random] Whether they are missing at random is exactly the question.
  - Because the sample becomes too small to analyse :: [precision-only] The loss of size matters less than the bias.
  hint: Who tends to drop out of a study?
  why: Missing data can bias results as well as weaken them. A protocol should plan how they will be handled and test how much the conclusion depends on that choice.
>P: A study of green space and wellbeing recruits volunteers in one coastal city. The report concludes that "access to green space improves wellbeing in cities everywhere". What is the criticism?
  + The claim goes beyond the design: volunteers in one city do not represent every city, and the design may not support a causal reading :: State the scope.
  - None: the result was significant :: [significance-as-generality] Significance says nothing about whom the result applies to.
  - The study is worthless :: [overcorrection] It is informative about its own setting, and that should be said.
  hint: Who was studied, where, and how were they chosen?
  why: The conclusions of a study extend as far as its sample, its setting and its design allow. Good reports state those limits.
done: Fix the question first, share what is safe, and claim only what the design supports. Read a protocol and find what was fixed in advance.
